# Backend egress allow-list (T16.0.4 added the Ollama rule only; T16.2.2/T16.2.4, 2026-11-25,
# self-reported CORRECTION below broadened it to every destination the backend actually needs).
# Templated like endpointslice.yaml.tmpl because the host IP is resolved fresh by
# scripts/k8s/cluster-up.sh on every (re)creation.
#
# CORRECTION (T16.2.2/T16.2.4, self-reported): the ORIGINAL comment here claimed "k3d's default
# CNI (flannel, via k3s) does NOT enforce NetworkPolicy" and that this object was a no-op until
# T16.4.1 installed a NetworkPolicy-capable CNI. That claim is FALSE on this cluster's actual k3s
# version (v1.35.5+k3s1, confirmed via `k3d version`) -- k3s now enforces NetworkPolicy out of the
# box. Proven live while building this block's chaos drills: the real backend image (not
# :placeholder) went CrashLoopBackOff with `OperationalError: [Errno -3] Temporary failure in
# name resolution` trying to reach Postgres; a raw UDP probe to kube-dns timed out and a raw TCP
# probe to both kube-dns:53 and secrag-pg-rw:5432 came back "Connection refused" (an active
# REJECT, not a route failure) -- `kubectl delete networkpolicy backend-egress-ollama` made both
# connect immediately, conclusively proving enforcement. The policy as originally written (ONLY
# Ollama:11434 allowed) therefore silently broke the backend's own DNS and Postgres egress the
# moment a real image replaced the placeholder -- never caught before because no real backend had
# ever run against this policy until this block built one. Broadened below to the backend's real
# egress set (DNS, Postgres/CNPG, SMTP/Mailpit, Prometheus, plus the existing Ollama rule) rather
# than reverted to "no effect assumed" -- T16.4 (block 16-E, Pod Security `restricted` + a
# namespace-wide default-deny + the OTHER services' own egress rules) still owns the complete,
# coordinated policy set; this fixes backend's own rule so it is correct TODAY, not a "no real
# enforcement yet" placeholder.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: backend-egress-ollama
  namespace: secrag
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/part-of: secrag
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app.kubernetes.io/name: backend
  policyTypes:
    - Egress
  egress:
    - to:
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: ${OLLAMA_HOST_IP}/32
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 11434
    # DNS (CoreDNS/kube-dns in kube-system) -- without this the backend cannot resolve ANY
    # in-cluster Service name, including Postgres (found live, see CORRECTION above).
    - to:
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system
          podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              k8s-app: kube-dns
      ports:
        - protocol: UDP
          port: 53
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 53
    # Postgres (CNPG's secrag-pg-rw/-ro/-r Services route to these pods).
    - to:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              cnpg.io/cluster: secrag-pg
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 5432
    # Mailpit (local SMTP sink, T12.1.11) and Prometheus (admin dashboard's MetricsBackend, T13.1.3).
    - to:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app.kubernetes.io/name: mailpit
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 1025
    - to:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app.kubernetes.io/name: prometheus
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 9090
